00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:43.640
in the ocean,

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:45.580
anything

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.725
related to water,
it's my happy place.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.130
So I'm back to
doing some kind of

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:58.550
going with my kids to
the pool and enjoying summertime.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.370
- That's great. Well, thank
you so much, Gabriela.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:02.450
And thank you for the

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:06.950
introduction to your role as
well. I know you mentioned AI

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:08.630
Inclusive a bit and R-Ladies.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:11.390
And many of us
might be familiar with R-Ladies,

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.960
not as familiar
with AI Inclusive.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.090
But would you be able to
just kind of describe what both of

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.410
those communities are?

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:19.880
- Yeah, so R-Ladies,

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:24.380
it's been almost 11 years
since I founded R-Ladies

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:29.780
here in San Francisco. And
the goal is to bring more

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.190
diversity into the R community.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:37.940
It all started here in San Francisco
but we have chapters, what we call

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:40.510
all over the world.

00:05:41.365 --> 00:05:47.370
I think we have over 200
chapters in more than 60-plus countries

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:49.720
with

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:51.990
thousands and
thousands of members.

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:57.650
And in each chapter they
organize and the way that works

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:02.490
best for them, it could be
like, they do workshops or they do

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.200
book clubs together or

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.360
they go through
lectures together.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:12.410
But the main thing is about
creating this community.

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:17.300
And you can see one thing
that I always mention is it's hard

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:21.640
to say about numbers,
how R-Ladies changing the ecosystem.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.730
The best way is to see
the pictures from the

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:29.720
conferences, how it was before
and how it is right now in

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:30.840
terms of diversity.

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:34.550
And then AI Inclusive,

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:39.544
it was another organization
that I created in 2018

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:45.750
when I was very much involved
in this AI discussion

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.050
about discrimination,

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:54.880
bias, and so on. And one of
the things that I noticed when we

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.180
were discussing those things is that
it was very US Silicon Valley centric,

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.670
all the discussions, and
the rest of the world,

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:06.850
they were not aware of
the implications of AI.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:08.950
And they were the ones--

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:11.080
like in some particular places,

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:13.480
they were the ones that were
going to suffer the most.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:13.980
So

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:17.553
my idea with the community
was kind of like twofold.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:21.550
The first one was to bring
awareness of the implications

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:24.290
of AI, and the second
one was to empower

00:07:26.275 --> 00:07:29.130
the underrepresented
community to

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:35.050
learn about the AI and
to have the tools to

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:39.550
make decisions and to be
knowledgeable about things that

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:42.220
are happening and also
entered the workforce.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.050
- Absolutely. Thank
you for sharing those.

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:47.200
I was going to put in the chat
if anybody wants to be the

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.180
first to share the links
to those two organizations in the

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:51.575
chat, that would
be helpful too.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.020
People are really
quick on that.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:56.240
But Gabriela,

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:59.180
something I really wanted to
ask you about and something we

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:01.100
spoke about a bit before

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:05.810
is the power in
building relationships within an

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:06.890
organization.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.980
And you shared with me how
you moved from a company

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:14.120
where everybody knew you to
having to then prove yourself

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:15.440
again in a new role.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:17.840
And I was wondering if you'd be
able to just share a little bit

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:19.910
about that experience with us.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:22.735
- Yeah, absolutely. So

00:08:23.455 --> 00:08:27.100
I left IBM, which is
a huge company. So

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:31.100
300,000 employees.

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:36.100
But I was there for a little
bit over four years and I knew

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:38.500
a bunch of people.
Because one of

00:08:39.847 --> 00:08:43.120
the things that I did with
my role was to connect with

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.560
different teams and working
with different teams.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:47.005
So I knew a bunch of people.

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:52.990
And then when I moved to Microsoft,
even on my team, they didn't know

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:56.650
about my past, the things
that I've done in the past.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:02.860
And not a lot of people inside
Microsoft knows who I am.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:05.830
So it's like, you need to

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:10.440
create your reputation,
your brand again

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.850
from scratch where you
have to prove yourself,

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:17.920
you have to show others your value.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:18.420
And

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:23.360
I think it's so difficult in that way.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:26.720
I thought it would be easier
but it's so hard because also

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:27.760
like, you don't
know the channels.

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:30.920
Which channels
should reach out.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:35.540
We use different tools, so you
have to get used to the tools.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:38.120
You need to find your
communities inside the company,

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:40.880
and it's not very
straightforward how to find

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:41.970
those communities.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:48.350
You need to build relationships again,
so it takes time. And I didn't--

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:49.460
I had no idea.

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:51.230
Maybe also

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:55.550
another reason could be because
everybody or most of us are

00:09:55.550 --> 00:10:00.800
remote, so we don't interface
with each other. So like,

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.430
there is no whole conversation or
seeing each other in the office,

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:07.290
getting to know their faces. So

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.190
it's been hard. It's
been hard. It's like,

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:15.830
it's one step after the other.
It's trying to even give talks

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:19.890
internally, seeing how I
can participate in other projects,

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:25.000
connect to people
one on one and so on.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:25.810
- I see

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:27.700
Luke put in the chat,

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:32.680
every career changer is nodding along
to the story. So do you have

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.230
any specific tips for people

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:37.390
of what you've experienced

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:40.585
in moving into Microsoft?

00:10:40.585 --> 00:10:46.300
- It's making sure that
you set time to go and

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:50.620
find people and schedule
one-on-ones. And then you ask

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:53.680
that person, oh, is there
anyone that I should meet?

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:55.705
And then it's
going this cascades

00:10:56.740 --> 00:11:00.640
motion. It's like, OK, I should
meet this other person. OK, oh,

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:02.125
there's this other
thing I should meet.

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:04.450
Oh, there is this group. Oh,

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:06.220
I should go and
talk to this group.

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:08.060
Oh, there is this other group

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:11.180
and so on.

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:12.760
So that would be my advice,

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:16.540
a lot of one-on-ones and doing
a lot of search inside the

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:19.285
tools that you have. Yeah,
it's snowball sampling,

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:21.380
it's snowball effect.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.380
That's pretty much

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:28.470
what I did, what
I've been doing.

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.785
And it's been almost
a year and I feel like

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:33.485
I still have a long way to go.

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:37.760
- Thank you. That's really
helpful. I see Dipsha,

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:39.080
you had asked a question.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.600
I think it was actually over
on Slido but I saw your name

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.160
there. So do you want
to ask that live?

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:49.340
- Hi, Gabriela. Always fun to hear
you talk about your work and your path.

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:53.210
One thing that I've always
admired about you is your

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:56.930
ability to constantly learn.
But especially as an advocate,

00:11:56.930 --> 00:12:00.800
how do you keep up with
the field that is growing faster

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:02.870
than our speed to
keep up with it?

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:04.928
Any advice for--

00:12:05.796 --> 00:12:10.220
- It is so hard because
it's faster than ever. It's

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.145
like, before the whole AI,

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:17.030
the generative AI and all
the tools and everything that it's

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:18.965
being born these days,

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:22.595
it was hard to keep
up, right? Now, it's

00:12:23.905 --> 00:12:27.670
I get very overwhelmed with
like, oh, should I know this?

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.520
Should I know that? Oh,
now there is this new tool.

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:37.500
So I try to focus as much
as I can and not being very

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:39.610
stressed about all
the new things,

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:41.920
because there is a
lot of noise as well.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:46.690
And making sure that I have
a network of people that I can

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:49.480
leverage them and their knowledge
so I can get my

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:52.870
knowledge as well instead of
me trying to figure out myself.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:54.340
So that has been

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:57.880
my tip or

00:12:58.740 --> 00:12:59.850
my suggestion

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:03.910
for myself because I used
to get very stressed. I'm like,

00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:05.470
I'm so behind. I mean,

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:09.220
this catch up mode all the
time and there is no ends.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:12.820
And I don't think I will
survive if I keep doing this.

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:17.420
So let me be a little bit
more smart and strategically.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:21.010
So I leverage my
peers, my co-workers.

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:27.500
Even you, Dipsha, we have some
calls every now and then to share

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:31.960
the things that she's doing with
R which I'm not doing as

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:32.990
much. And I'm like,

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:36.370
I want to know what is
happening in the world of R.

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:39.100
And so we have some
conversations. So it's

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:40.330
leveraging people

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:46.765
is the best way that I found to
be up to date on what is happening.

00:13:46.765 --> 00:13:51.700
- I like to remind everybody
because I forget to say this

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:54.730
again. If you join after
the initial introductions,

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:57.050
there's a few different
ways you can ask questions.

00:13:57.050 --> 00:14:00.070
So I see people have figured
out you can write in the chat

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:02.830
here on Zoom. But if you
also wanted to jump in live,

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:06.790
you can raise your hand on Zoom
or use the Slido link to ask

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.940
anonymously, which Curtis
will share again in a second.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:11.640
But

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:14.370
S. Moorthy,

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:18.320
S. Moorthy, I see you had
asked a question here in the chat.

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:19.770
Do you want to jump in?

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.405
S. MOORTHY: Sure.
Sorry, my camera's not working.

00:14:22.405 --> 00:14:23.230
- No worries.

00:14:25.075 --> 00:14:25.750
S. MOORTHY: So

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:31.990
i am a pure biologist by
trade but I've really started

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:36.025
enjoying data science and really
see my future in that field.

00:14:36.820 --> 00:14:41.600
And I always wonder,
because now both Microsoft, Amazon,

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:44.965
Google are all jumping
on the genomics boat and

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:48.840
would entertain one of these
companies be a potential

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:51.775
a job for me.

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:53.890
So in your experience,

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:58.270
how do you deal with or
manage people who come from really

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:00.940
different backgrounds that
might not be traditionally

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:05.035
computational or traditionally
statistics or math?

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:08.890
And how do you actually
leverage their knowledge in

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:10.960
your work environment?

00:15:11.826 --> 00:15:15.440
- Yeah, that's a great
question. And when I'm hiring,

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:16.490
I'm looking for

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:18.330
a broad

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:23.420
set of skills. Like,
I try to make it--

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:26.660
I say that it's kind
of like a puzzle.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:28.430
It's like bringing
together a team,

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:30.520
it's like building
this puzzle where

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:34.440
I need people with
different backgrounds,

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:36.580
different set of skills,
different set of ideas,

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:40.260
different ways of
working and thinking.

00:15:41.210 --> 00:15:44.180
And I feel like the academic,

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:46.865
if you have an academic
background, no matter which one,

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:51.860
it's also very helpful and you
bring a lot of expertise and

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:55.400
deep knowledge that maybe other
professionals don't have as

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:58.740
much or vice versa.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:00.500
One thing that I think--

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:04.640
and you don't have to
have the traditional like statistical

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:06.395
background or computer science.

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:10.770
It's well proven that doesn't,

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:13.880
what do you say?

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:19.340
The best teams are
the most diverse ones.

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:23.885
And I do believe that a lot.

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:29.915
And I've been building several teams
for the past few years, and

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:31.580
I saw

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:34.415
through my teams. And

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:37.730
so there are a lot
of opportunities.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:42.590
And the other thing that I
think about is like, if there

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:44.570
is an area that
you are an expert,

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:45.980
that you feel like
you are an expert,

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:48.025
try to find a job
that you can relate.

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:49.790
And

00:16:51.485 --> 00:16:52.890
I'll give an example.

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:56.990
So I was in academia for
several years before moving to

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:59.300
the industry. And
it was hard for me

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:04.414
to make the bridge between the
academic world and the industry world.

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:09.770
So once I was talking to
people to try to understand,

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:11.705
one of the things
that I realized is

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:14.450
we all have the same--

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:18.890
kind of a similar knowledge but
the name, the way that we name

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:20.630
things, the terminology,

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:23.730
they are so different but
they mean the same thing.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:28.040
Once I was able to have that
clicked, I was like, OK,

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:29.720
so you are talking
about this. Oh yeah,

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:32.960
I know what this thing is.
Oh, this thing is this, this,

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.825
and this that I learned when
I was doing my research.

00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:40.205
Oh, the model that you're
using in the industry is called

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:43.800
this other thing. Oh,
yeah, I know what it means.

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:47.660
So once I was able to
make this connection,

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:50.150
it became much easier.

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:53.105
- What's the biggest
thing that happens with

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:55.820
where I mean,

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:59.420
we call them two totally different
things but they mean the same things.

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:00.425
- Say it again.

00:18:00.425 --> 00:18:02.595
- Where do you see
that happening the Most

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:04.490
- Oh,

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:08.603
even statistics and
machine learning, for example,

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:13.730
that was the first
insight. Like, oh yeah. Or

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:19.240
if you go to a certain
industry like advertisement,

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:23.030
they have a lot of acronyms,
they love acronyms.

00:18:23.030 --> 00:18:26.550
And then you have to understand
what the acronyms mean.

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:29.750
And then you're like, OK,
I know what it is.

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:32.210
- Yeah, definitely. Libby,

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:34.290
I see you had a question
here in the chat.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:35.150
Do you want to jump in?

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:36.910
- Sure.

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:40.330
I was wondering because
you mentioned buy versus build

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:43.015
questions for the startups
that you work with.

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:46.090
I was wondering if there
are any other problems that you

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:49.480
could kind of describe
that these startup companies are

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:53.140
facing in this landscape and kind
of how your team helps. Like,

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:55.630
is it a lot of
research where you're researching things and

00:18:55.630 --> 00:18:58.930
then bringing knowledge back
to your clients or is it

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:01.360
relationship building with
experts and then bringing those

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:04.150
experts in to talk to the
clients? What does that look like?

00:19:05.192 --> 00:19:09.180
- Yeah, so let me talk about
the first one. There is a lot

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:11.280
of noise right now
in the AI space.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:14.640
A lot of startups being
created every day.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:16.830
One of the things that I see

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:18.470
happening

00:19:18.990 --> 00:19:23.295
very often is like, they haven't done

00:19:23.985 --> 00:19:25.120
the kind of research

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.080
side of knowing that,

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:30.270
is there any other company that
is doing the same thing that

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:32.760
I'm trying to build?
So there is like,

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:36.750
the same thing being built over
and over and over again with

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:40.665
just a different kind of look and feel.

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:42.450
And

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:46.650
so there is this market
research that they haven't

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:48.930
done, so I would
call it the homework.

00:19:48.930 --> 00:19:50.860
Making sure that
what you are doing,

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:53.790
there is a value behind
and that you have a value

00:19:53.790 --> 00:19:57.930
proposition. So I usually
go to some events and I

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:59.640
say, oh, "Give me your pitch.

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:03.360
Tell me about your company."
And they were like, "O yeah,

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:05.595
we have this wrapper
around the OpenAI,

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:07.280
API,

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:10.120
and you get a model."

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:13.155
And I'm like, "OK, but what
is the new thing here?"

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:18.250
"Oh, not much." And I'm
like, "OK, who are your customers?

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:22.435
Who are you targeting?" "Oh,
I haven't thought about this." So

00:20:23.410 --> 00:20:25.450
there is a lot of
things that I feel like

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:28.060
it's missing on the startup.

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:31.150
And the startups that I'm
talking to, they are in the

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:34.150
very, very, very,
very early stages.

00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:34.650
Usually,

00:20:36.742 --> 00:20:38.965
they don't have any

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:40.260
funding,

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.890
they are not backed
by any VC or anyone,

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:46.060
they are just starting.

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:49.750
It could be like a one-person
startup or it can be like a

00:20:49.750 --> 00:20:50.950
five people startup.

00:20:51.790 --> 00:20:55.780
It can be an idea as well
and they are thinking of like

00:20:56.680 --> 00:21:02.530
incorporating or becoming a startup.
So that's the first challenge.

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:06.730
The other question that
you mentioned about the knowledge.

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:08.890
So yes, we do--

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:13.030
not like research in the way
that we do is like knowing what

00:21:13.030 --> 00:21:14.245
is happening in this space

00:21:15.370 --> 00:21:17.230
and also like understanding,

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:19.650
what is the value that
we bring as Microsoft?

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:25.300
How we compare with other
companies as well. And we know

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:27.160
that those startups.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:28.460
And as they progress

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:30.640
or even in the beginning,

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:33.340
they are not going to
be building in one cloud.

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:35.650
They are not going to be
building on only Microsoft,

00:21:35.650 --> 00:21:39.580
they are probably going to be
building in this what we call multicloud

00:21:40.390 --> 00:21:43.690
environment. So they are going
to be using Microsoft Cloud, AWS,

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:46.360
Google Cloud platform.

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:51.130
And how can we incorporate
the other clouds,

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:54.460
make sure that they
have a good workflow?

00:21:54.460 --> 00:21:54.960
So

00:21:55.510 --> 00:22:00.250
we also research what the other
companies are doing but also like,

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:03.115
what are the advantages that
we are offering to our startup

00:22:04.540 --> 00:22:05.320
ecosystem?

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:08.860
And there is a team.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:13.300
It's not only my team but there
is several and different teams working with

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:18.115
the Microsoft Word
startup teams.

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:21.150
- How do you find like, these

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:24.630
early stage
startups to talk to?

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:27.780
I guess I'm thinking as
you were telling that story like,

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:28.980
if I was a startup,

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:32.190
I would probably need to feel like
this might just be how I am,

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:35.610
like too prepared for that
conversation before I'm talking

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:38.655
to Microsoft. Like, how do
you first get connected with them?

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:40.100
- So

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:46.500
a few ways. One is we're
going to events and talking to

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:49.650
them. And it could be a
one-on-one or us being on a

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:51.660
stage or us having a booth.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:54.450
But also, we have a platform.

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:57.675
So we have something
called Founders Hub,

00:22:58.650 --> 00:22:59.740
which is a platform

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:03.480
that any startup,
anyone with an idea

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:06.000
