I was a

00:29:16.570 --> 00:29:21.790
heavy user of Twitter and then
people migrate to one and then

00:29:21.790 --> 00:29:25.000
migrate to the other one,
and then I lost track.

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:27.640
I don't know where
the community is anymore

00:29:28.300 --> 00:29:29.390
so I'm like, OK,

00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:31.720
I'm going to stay with LinkedIn
but I know that some people

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:33.470
is there.

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:39.740
But I haven't found my
home again. I'm still on Twitter

00:29:40.460 --> 00:29:43.280
but I haven't found like,
a social media that works

00:29:44.060 --> 00:29:48.020
where I feel like the community
that we used to have on Twitter.

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:50.840
- Any ideas, feel free
to share in the chat.

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:51.770
I've definitely been

00:29:52.400 --> 00:29:55.370
heavy on the LinkedIn
but always on LinkedIn.

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:56.015
- Blue sky,

00:29:56.840 --> 00:29:59.510
I haven't used it.
I have Macedon.

00:30:00.740 --> 00:30:01.240
But

00:30:02.270 --> 00:30:05.840
it took me a while to
understand, and I'm not--

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:10.410
I don't know, I don't feel
like I'm comfortable with the UI,

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:12.230
so I go always back to Twitter.

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:14.780
But then the community is not
there, then I go to LinkedIn.

00:30:14.780 --> 00:30:17.000
So it's a mess. I
would say it's a mess.

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:21.050
I do miss our community in the
way that we use to share knowledge.

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:26.135
- A question I had wanted
to ask you about is

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:29.420
thinking about R-Ladies

00:30:30.080 --> 00:30:31.580
and AI Inclusive,

00:30:33.410 --> 00:30:36.630
how have you helped
those organizations grow?

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:39.470
It's amazing to see how
many different chapters of R-Ladies

00:30:39.470 --> 00:30:41.405
there are across the
world, for example.

00:30:42.766 --> 00:30:45.740
- One of the things
that helped us for sure

00:30:47.865 --> 00:30:50.345
was coming from
being a one person

00:30:52.310 --> 00:30:56.870
working on the organization to being
a team and to have money

00:30:56.870 --> 00:31:02.060
so we had funding. So
that's where R-Ladies started to grow.

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:07.010
But I would say that not only
having a team and funding

00:31:07.010 --> 00:31:10.310
is also making sure that you
are taking care of your community.

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:15.140
I remember for several years, I
would go to meetups and events,

00:31:15.140 --> 00:31:16.265
and I would handle

00:31:17.090 --> 00:31:21.455
a little card with not only
my name but the R-Ladies

00:31:22.190 --> 00:31:23.940
website. And I would say, hey,

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:26.180
do you know about
this community? It's awesome.

00:31:26.180 --> 00:31:27.395
And you should check it out.

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:32.540
So it was a word of mouth
by myself doing this type of thing.

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:39.020
But it really changed when
we had a team behind and the

00:31:39.020 --> 00:31:42.770
funding, which came first
from the AI consortium.

00:31:42.770 --> 00:31:43.580
- Thank you.

00:31:44.540 --> 00:31:46.700
Allen, I see you had a
question here in the chat.

00:31:46.700 --> 00:31:47.825
Want to jump in?

00:31:47.825 --> 00:31:51.180
ALLEN: Yeah. Hi, Gabriela.
This is a great conversation,

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:54.330
thank you. I'm
curious if Microsoft

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:58.150
is doing anything
institutionally to promote

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:01.800
solutions for things like
algorithmic bias and AI issues

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:03.015
with training

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:05.595
data shaped by
biases and so on.

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:08.280
And I also wonder if working
in the startup space,

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:10.320
if there are
opportunities for you to

00:32:11.736 --> 00:32:14.370
be the change that you want
to see in the world with respect

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:19.020
to inclusion in AI communities,
people doing AI but also in

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:20.730
the data, in the
foundational stuff.

00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:22.410
Are there
opportunities for you?

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:26.430
Do you see levers that you
can pull in that space there?

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:27.990
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:32:27.990 --> 00:32:32.340
And Microsoft has a
whole organization around this

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:33.910
particular theme

00:32:34.590 --> 00:32:35.850
around responsible AI.

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:38.190
I don't know the name of

00:32:39.990 --> 00:32:42.520
the organization, but
they have several--

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:44.325
not only like

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:47.670
well-known researchers
doing research

00:32:48.510 --> 00:32:50.850
but people working with
customers to make sure that

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:54.334
they have this responsible AI principles

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:57.480
embedded into the system.

00:32:57.480 --> 00:33:01.445
When we talk about startups,
we also emphasize the

00:33:02.270 --> 00:33:05.960
responsibility that they have
and the way that they can

00:33:06.860 --> 00:33:09.860
mitigate bias, that they can
be more knowledgeable about

00:33:10.660 --> 00:33:14.990
this topic. And in the
product itself, we have it imbibed.

00:33:15.740 --> 00:33:17.165
So startups,

00:33:18.050 --> 00:33:21.020
some of them, they like, oh,
I don't want to know about this.

00:33:21.020 --> 00:33:25.250
But it's our role as being
part of Microsoft, educate

00:33:25.250 --> 00:33:27.005
them about this as well.

00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:30.280
Even if they don't
want to hear or listen

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:32.270
about this but it's

00:33:33.665 --> 00:33:35.870
in our responsibility to be

00:33:36.730 --> 00:33:39.920
that person that's like oh
yeah, you have to listen to

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:41.480
this. It's important.

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:43.100
And sometimes they
don't know. Like,

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:45.980
they were not educated or

00:33:46.660 --> 00:33:51.080
they didn't know about those problems
because they never had any issues.

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:52.390
But

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:56.760
it's part of our let's
say speech when we talk.

00:33:58.022 --> 00:33:59.100
ALLEN: Great. Thanks.

00:33:59.100 --> 00:34:01.250
I appreciate hearing that
that's sort of embedded in the

00:34:01.250 --> 00:34:03.750
approach. I think that's
really important going forward.

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:04.595
Thank you.

00:34:04.595 --> 00:34:06.400
- It is. And the whole company,

00:34:07.270 --> 00:34:11.610
we had to go through several
trainings as well. And

00:34:12.550 --> 00:34:13.630
there is a lot of

00:34:15.031 --> 00:34:18.140
resources for us. And how to

00:34:19.449 --> 00:34:24.250
learn, where to learn, how to
talk about it, what are some

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:26.619
resources that could
be helpful for you

00:34:27.409 --> 00:34:28.750
and so on.

00:34:28.750 --> 00:34:33.250
- I like the tip you
shared about the business cards with

00:34:33.250 --> 00:34:35.845
the community. And I
was just thinking about,

00:34:35.845 --> 00:34:37.749
a lot of us who
might be going to

00:34:38.860 --> 00:34:41.710
conferences again where we
haven't been to them in a few

00:34:41.710 --> 00:34:45.940
years, do you have other advice
or tips for people to get the

00:34:45.940 --> 00:34:48.084
most out of conferences?

00:34:48.084 --> 00:34:49.134
- And that was--

00:34:49.134 --> 00:34:52.180
I just wrote in the
chat, that was before stickers because

00:34:53.949 --> 00:34:56.469
I think stickers would
be easier to handle,

00:34:57.130 --> 00:35:02.430
to hand and to give people.
But back then, we didn't have this

00:35:03.130 --> 00:35:03.630
sticker

00:35:04.090 --> 00:35:05.455
kind of like, fever

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:09.120
conferences.

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:14.540
Well, in my opinion, the
best thing at conferences is the

00:35:14.540 --> 00:35:19.070
talk, like the whole
conversation. More than talks

00:35:19.070 --> 00:35:21.770
is the conversation
and reconnecting with people.

00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:25.685
So usually when I go to
conferences, I pick some talks

00:35:26.810 --> 00:35:29.370
that I'm like oh,
I can't miss those.

00:35:30.050 --> 00:35:31.490
This other one, OK,

00:35:32.960 --> 00:35:34.550
maybe I can. But like,

00:35:35.290 --> 00:35:38.090
the whole conversation, the
connections that I make,

00:35:38.090 --> 00:35:41.240
every time I go to
conferences, it's priceless.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:44.970
And that's why I go to
conferences. It's not about the talks,

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:46.560
it's about

00:35:47.540 --> 00:35:48.545
the people.

00:35:49.726 --> 00:35:54.060
- Let me just check here to
see if I missed questions in

00:35:54.060 --> 00:35:56.170
the chat here.

00:35:56.170 --> 00:35:58.995
I know there's a lot
of conversation happening about the

00:35:59.940 --> 00:36:02.415
different social
networks and stuff here.

00:36:03.010 --> 00:36:03.990
Let's see. But

00:36:04.590 --> 00:36:07.460
if I missed one of your
questions there, feel free to recopy

00:36:08.340 --> 00:36:09.915
and paste it so
we see it again.

00:36:11.610 --> 00:36:15.570
But a question that I love
to ask our featured leaders here,

00:36:15.570 --> 00:36:18.345
Gabriela, is is there a

00:36:19.540 --> 00:36:22.300
piece of career advice
that comes to mind,

00:36:22.300 --> 00:36:25.280
whether you've been the one to
give it or that you've received

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:29.020
that you think is
very impactful?

00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:32.725
- I would say is the one about

00:36:33.850 --> 00:36:37.360
applying for a job even if
you feel like you are not qualified,

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:41.920
because that's the starting
point. If you don't apply,

00:36:43.450 --> 00:36:46.420
there is no way that
it can get that role.

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:48.480
So that would be the
first one, is apply,

00:36:49.200 --> 00:36:50.860
apply as much as you can,

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:52.553
even if you feel like
you don't qualify.

00:36:53.320 --> 00:36:55.380
So that would be my first one.

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:56.850
- That's a great one.

00:36:59.130 --> 00:37:00.930
On the conference conversation,

00:37:00.930 --> 00:37:05.430
I see somebody asked a Slido
question that was, do you have

00:37:05.430 --> 00:37:08.190
any networking tips for
the socially anxious?

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:10.830
The thought of handing out
cards or stickers makes me

00:37:10.830 --> 00:37:12.135
break out in a cold sweat.

00:37:14.244 --> 00:37:17.700
- Let me share something
interesting. So I was not--

00:37:17.700 --> 00:37:20.970
and I don't consider
myself being an extrovert

00:37:22.350 --> 00:37:22.850
person.

00:37:23.400 --> 00:37:27.330
I think if in a place
where I feel comfortable, then I can

00:37:27.330 --> 00:37:32.550
be that person. But like, if I
go to a place, an event where I

00:37:32.550 --> 00:37:36.075
don't know anyone, it's
so hard to break the ice.

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:40.050
But it's been an exercise
that I've been doing for

00:37:40.890 --> 00:37:42.300
well, let's say

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:44.680
probably 20 years.

00:37:46.400 --> 00:37:51.020
And because one of the things
that I wanted to change in myself

00:37:51.700 --> 00:37:56.630
was to be that person
that would talk to others. And

00:37:57.700 --> 00:37:58.840
it always started--

00:37:58.840 --> 00:37:59.830
and I'm going back in time,

00:37:59.830 --> 00:38:04.450
just to share that I
was very anxious about being surrounded

00:38:04.450 --> 00:38:07.630
by people and being the
center of attention.

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:13.090
But for several years,
I worked with some music

00:38:13.090 --> 00:38:14.905
bands and singers,

00:38:16.330 --> 00:38:18.490
and I was doing the
production side.

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:25.210
So I would have to go and
hand flyers and go and talk to

00:38:25.210 --> 00:38:26.620
the press about the

00:38:27.400 --> 00:38:28.430
upcoming concerts.

00:38:29.230 --> 00:38:32.120
And then I remember talking
to someone and I'm like,

00:38:32.120 --> 00:38:34.960
this is so hard for me.
And then someone told me,

00:38:34.960 --> 00:38:37.060
"Is there anyone
that you admire?

00:38:37.060 --> 00:38:41.200
That is that person that you
would like to be in terms of

00:38:41.200 --> 00:38:44.780
that specific skills?" And
I'm like, "Yes, there is."

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:49.310
So I would see that person
and I would try to see how

00:38:49.310 --> 00:38:52.970
they behaved, how they interact,
and I would try to mimic

00:38:53.960 --> 00:38:55.490
and my hands would be sweating.

00:38:56.790 --> 00:38:58.085
And I would be like

00:38:59.240 --> 00:39:03.835
not being able to talk very
much. And my ideas would all blank.

00:39:05.090 --> 00:39:07.295
But anyway, all
that to say that

00:39:08.540 --> 00:39:12.470
I don't think it's like
some of us are extroverts.

00:39:12.470 --> 00:39:16.100
By the end of a conference
or mid day of a conference,

00:39:16.100 --> 00:39:20.210
I go back to my room and
I'd be quiet because I can't talk to

00:39:20.210 --> 00:39:23.150
people anymore. And then I recharge
and then I can go back.

00:39:24.260 --> 00:39:27.170
But try maybe the
one-on-one. Like,

00:39:27.170 --> 00:39:29.150
instead of talking
to a lot of people,

00:39:29.150 --> 00:39:33.800
I prefer usually to do
one-on-ones than to have a

00:39:33.800 --> 00:39:35.540
group of people. One,

00:39:35.540 --> 00:39:40.730
because it's a lot of noise
and I can't project my voice in

00:39:40.730 --> 00:39:44.450
that way to people to listen
to me. So I really like the

00:39:44.450 --> 00:39:48.890
one-on-one in the corner, and I
feel like I take more out of

00:39:48.890 --> 00:39:51.560
these one-on-ones than
a group conversation.

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:52.760
- Definitely.

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:57.140
I'll share something that has been
useful for me with that is

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:02.510
if I see that somebody
posted on LinkedIn or wherever that

00:40:02.510 --> 00:40:04.070
they're going to be
at that conference,

00:40:04.070 --> 00:40:06.900
I'll connect with them
and just say like, hey,

00:40:06.900 --> 00:40:10.250
I saw you are also going
to this conference or like, hey,

00:40:10.250 --> 00:40:13.040
looking forward to seeing
you at Posit's conference.

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:17.480
And it just makes me
feel more comfortable than saying hello

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:20.540
to them in person because
of already reached out to them

00:40:20.540 --> 00:40:24.740
before. And in my mind I
have an idea of maybe 5 to 10

00:40:24.740 --> 00:40:27.830
people who are going to seem
familiar to me that I could

00:40:27.830 --> 00:40:31.350
then talk to. I don't
know if that's helpful for anybody.

00:40:31.350 --> 00:40:33.780
I see Libby you had an
idea in the chat too.

00:40:33.780 --> 00:40:34.970
Do you want to jump in?

00:40:34.970 --> 00:40:37.820
- Yeah, so I'm a
big introvert who

00:40:38.540 --> 00:40:42.050
talks to a lot of people
and has a podcast and does things

00:40:42.050 --> 00:40:44.150
that are not
introvert-like at all.

00:40:45.200 --> 00:40:45.700
And

00:40:46.370 --> 00:40:49.010
I give myself a job. Like,
I volunteer for something.

00:40:49.010 --> 00:40:52.010
Can I help you with something?
Can I sign up for something?

00:40:52.010 --> 00:40:55.370
Something that gives me a
purpose and then I know what

00:40:55.370 --> 00:40:57.200
I'm doing. I have
an objective, right?

00:40:57.200 --> 00:40:58.550
And I also have
something in my hands,

00:40:58.550 --> 00:41:00.290
I always bring my
camera. That way,

00:41:00.290 --> 00:41:01.910
I have a reason to
talk to somebody,

00:41:01.910 --> 00:41:04.550
they have a reason to talk to me,
I have a reason to walk away. Like, oh,

00:41:04.550 --> 00:41:06.830
I got to go take a picture
of those people and then walk away.

00:41:07.850 --> 00:41:10.760
Just gives me an
object that I can

00:41:12.164 --> 00:41:14.300
use as a focal point.

00:41:14.300 --> 00:41:17.840
And then my other piece
of advice is to practice. Like,

00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:20.450
we all have this strange
assumption that we're supposed

00:41:20.450 --> 00:41:21.710
to know how to talk to people.

00:41:22.460 --> 00:41:24.170
No, not everyone does.

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:26.270
And we just went through
a pandemic where we weren't

00:41:26.270 --> 00:41:29.465
getting as much FaceTime as
we normally maybe would have. So

00:41:30.080 --> 00:41:33.110
figure out how to phrase things
and then practice them out loud

00:41:33.110 --> 00:41:35.255
in the mirror or whatever
it takes. Give yourself

00:41:35.990 --> 00:41:37.670
a little bit of help.

00:41:37.670 --> 00:41:37.730
-

00:41:37.730 --> 00:41:40.850
I love that idea of giving
yourself a job and just like

00:41:40.850 --> 00:41:44.060
carrying your camera and
making your own job there.

00:41:45.310 --> 00:41:48.910
I think that helped as
being like a community organizer too

00:41:48.910 --> 00:41:50.260
for local meetup groups,

00:41:50.260 --> 00:41:54.160
because you always did have something you
had to go run and do as well.

00:41:56.330 --> 00:41:56.890
I see--

00:41:57.390 --> 00:41:58.170
oh, there you go.

00:41:58.170 --> 00:41:59.590
- No, that's a great tip.

00:42:00.220 --> 00:42:02.620
And I have a huge--

00:42:02.620 --> 00:42:07.360
I'm very self-conscious. And
no matter how many people told

00:42:07.360 --> 00:42:09.900
me otherwise with

00:42:11.020 --> 00:42:15.070
my English. Like, I've
been in the US for

00:42:15.790 --> 00:42:20.620
11, 12 years, and I
still struggle with English. And

00:42:20.620 --> 00:42:23.210
everybody says no, I
can understand you perfectly.

00:42:23.210 --> 00:42:25.880
But every day, it's a
struggle. And I'm like, OK,

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:28.490
I'm going to fight this. I'm
going to get on the phone.

00:42:28.490 --> 00:42:30.130
I'm going to make
this appointments.

00:42:30.130 --> 00:42:31.690
I'm going to try to--

00:42:31.690 --> 00:42:32.190
So

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:33.520
it's not natural.

00:42:33.520 --> 00:42:34.020
Again,

00:42:34.750 --> 00:42:36.963
it can be in other
forms as well,

00:42:37.670 --> 00:42:39.400
especially if you don't
speak the language

00:42:40.690 --> 00:42:44.260
or if you are very conscious
about the way that you speak

00:42:44.260 --> 00:42:49.030
and you cannot express yourself the way
that you would like to, it can be

00:42:50.020 --> 00:42:51.520
frustrating as well.

00:42:51.520 --> 00:42:52.100
- Yeah,

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:54.020
absolutely.

00:42:55.840 --> 00:42:59.170
Saptarishi. And apologies if
I mispronounce anybody's name.

00:42:59.170 --> 00:43:00.770
I see you had asked a
question in the chat.

00:43:00.770 --> 00:43:01.645
Do you to jump in here?

00:43:03.121 --> 00:43:05.960
- Hi. You got my pronunciation
right, you're fine.

00:43:05.960 --> 00:43:07.475
- Oh, thank you.

00:43:07.475 --> 00:43:08.015
- Yeah.

00:43:09.110 --> 00:43:12.860
So I'm a marketing analytics
student here at Simon Business School,

00:43:12.860 --> 00:43:15.830
but I've found myself really
interested in data science over

00:43:15.830 --> 00:43:16.550
the past one year.

00:43:16.550 --> 00:43:19.220
And I've been getting really more
and more into it more like practice.

00:43:20.210 --> 00:43:23.180
And I've had a few
portfolio worthy projects but with the

00:43:23.180 --> 00:43:26.900
kind of market situation and how
it is in the professional environment,

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:30.450
I'm really struggling and wondering how
I should like break into it.

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:34.040
And I always wonder if I'm
like good enough yet or not because

00:43:34.040 --> 00:43:35.840
there's always
something new to learn.

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:36.960
There's always a new tool.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:39.440
So I was wondering if there
is something that I could

00:43:39.440 --> 00:43:41.510
particularly do that's
probably going to help me out.

00:43:42.746 --> 00:43:46.490
- Yeah, there is
always something to learn. Like,

00:43:46.490 --> 00:43:47.990
you can ask a few questions--

00:43:47.990 --> 00:43:49.730
like, not a few,

00:43:49.730 --> 00:43:53.420
several questions you may know and
I will say I don't know but

00:43:53.420 --> 00:43:54.875
I can probably figure out.

00:43:55.550 --> 00:43:57.410
It's impossible to
know everything.

00:43:57.410 --> 00:43:57.910
So

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:02.240
my suggestion is always like,
if you come from marketing,

00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:04.620
if you come from
biology, if you come from

00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:10.500
epidemiology, try to find a job
where you can leverage those skills

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:11.780
so

00:44:13.030 --> 00:44:14.550
you know the domain.

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:17.610
So you also know data
science but maybe

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:20.680
the person that is hiring

00:44:21.630 --> 00:44:23.298
cannot see how much you know

00:44:24.114 --> 00:44:26.050
because you don't
have experience,

00:44:26.050 --> 00:44:28.350
work experience with data
science but you have knowledge

00:44:28.350 --> 00:44:30.135
about marketing.
So we can try to

00:44:31.274 --> 00:44:31.774
highlight

00:44:32.535 --> 00:44:36.540
the pieces that you
know more and find

00:44:38.148 --> 00:44:40.620
a job that is more
focused in that area.

00:44:40.620 --> 00:44:43.005
And then you can
start from that place.

00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:44.390
Another

00:44:44.910 --> 00:44:45.960
suggestion would be

00:44:46.785 --> 00:44:48.420
the first job is

00:44:49.260 --> 00:44:52.010
the hardest for sure. The first
one is going to be the hardest.

00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:54.940
I'm not saying that
the second, the third,

00:44:54.940 --> 00:44:56.370
the fourth and the
fifth, it's easy.

00:44:57.376 --> 00:44:57.876
I

00:44:58.890 --> 00:45:00.830
got a lot of rejections

00:45:02.420 --> 00:45:06.095
when I was applying to
every time I changed jobs.

00:45:07.990 --> 00:45:09.930
It's like I get a
lot of rejection,

00:45:09.930 --> 00:45:11.765
so I don't think it ever ends.

00:45:12.590 --> 00:45:15.140
But I would say the
first one is the hardest.

00:45:15.920 --> 00:45:19.340
Even if you have to do
two steps back and start

00:45:20.590 --> 00:45:21.835
in a more like beginner

00:45:23.960 --> 00:45:25.280
role, that's fine

00:45:26.120 --> 00:45:28.730
because I see that
it's an investment.

00:45:29.850 --> 00:45:34.835
So that would be my suggestion.
And apply. Apply, apply, apply, because

00:45:35.765 --> 00:45:39.110
that's the way the
industry works is like,

00:45:39.110 --> 00:45:41.870
you have to apply as much
as you can. And it's ridiculous,

00:45:41.870 --> 00:45:47.285
you apply to 200, 300 jobs
sometimes and you get maybe 5,

00:45:47.285 --> 00:45:49.250
10 interviews if you are lucky.

00:45:49.250 --> 00:45:51.005
So keep applying,

00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:54.620
and I'm sure you'll get
something very soon.

00:45:55.240 --> 00:45:57.470
- Thank you.

00:45:57.470 --> 00:46:00.740
And when you were talking about
that first job being the hardest,

00:46:00.740 --> 00:46:06.160
it was just reminding me you're
talking about your job in music production.

00:46:06.160 --> 00:46:09.240
And I would just love to hear
a little bit about your early

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:12.720
career journey or how
you got into data.

00:46:12.720 --> 00:46:15.330
- Oh, that's a
long, long story.

00:46:20.270 --> 00:46:21.290
So first of all,

00:46:25.200 --> 00:46:26.690
that was back in Brazil.

00:46:26.690 --> 00:46:30.250
So I didn't know exactly
what I want to do or

00:46:30.880 --> 00:46:33.100
what major I wanted to do.

00:46:33.100 --> 00:46:36.435
So I did a lot of back and
forth and trying other things

00:46:37.300 --> 00:46:41.410
and not doing anything but
working with music because it

00:46:41.410 --> 00:46:43.345
was something that I
was passionate about.

00:46:45.510 --> 00:46:47.265
So there is a lot
of back and forth

00:46:48.390 --> 00:46:49.095
in my career.

00:46:49.830 --> 00:46:54.300
And I say that it's never
too late for you to apply

00:46:55.830 --> 00:46:58.320
and work in a place that
you want to work for.

00:46:59.700 --> 00:47:01.440
So I finished my bachelor.

00:47:02.310 --> 00:47:04.395
I always say that
I was almost 30.

00:47:05.490 --> 00:47:09.510
And I think in the past,
even my family, they were like oh,

00:47:09.510 --> 00:47:11.880
I don't know what's going
to happen to Gabriela because

00:47:11.880 --> 00:47:15.750
she's trying all these other
things and she's not finishing

00:47:15.750 --> 00:47:19.005
school. Like, she's trying
these different majors

00:47:20.670 --> 00:47:21.483
and stopping.

00:47:23.970 --> 00:47:28.320
But then I found my way.
Then I found something that I really

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:33.045
fell in love, which was statistics.
But even then it took me

00:47:33.720 --> 00:47:35.550
forever to finish my course.

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:44.925
So anyway, all that to say
that it's never too late.

00:47:46.245 --> 00:47:49.200
All the experience that you have
done in the past is going

00:47:49.200 --> 00:47:53.100
to be helpful in some
ways. Like, I think that

00:47:54.580 --> 00:47:57.420
starting a community
and being an advocate,

00:47:58.100 --> 00:47:59.305
those two things,

00:48:00.190 --> 00:48:04.690
when I was working with music,
if I haven't worked with music,

00:48:04.690 --> 00:48:08.680
I'm not sure if I was able
to do those two things because a

00:48:08.680 --> 00:48:11.800
lot of skills that I developed
when I was doing the music

00:48:11.800 --> 00:48:16.570
production helped me with these
two pieces and being able to

00:48:16.570 --> 00:48:19.345
talk and communicate
because I had to do this.

00:48:20.630 --> 00:48:25.030
So nothing that you do is
lose, like, don't loss.

00:48:25.030 --> 00:48:28.780
I don't believe that it's like
there is this run that you have

00:48:28.780 --> 00:48:33.430
to finish and you have to
start programming at the age of five

00:48:33.430 --> 00:48:36.870
to be someone
successful by 30. Maybe,

00:48:37.480 --> 00:48:40.810
sure, yeah but I
don't regret any loops

00:48:42.280 --> 00:48:43.540
that I did.

00:48:43.540 --> 00:48:43.990
-

00:48:43.990 --> 00:48:47.620
I absolutely love hearing
about those lessons learned and

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:51.340
different career paths and how
that influences you and the

00:48:51.340 --> 00:48:52.690
role that you have now too.

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:59.855
What was that moment where you
fell in love with statistics? Like, why?

00:49:01.400 --> 00:49:02.440
- So that was

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:07.390
when I saw that things were--

00:49:07.390 --> 00:49:08.380
even like the

00:49:09.430 --> 00:49:14.680
news on TV, they were
talking about data, statistics, data,

00:49:14.680 --> 00:49:19.090
statistics. And I'm like,
ha, so statistics is much

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:22.600
more than math. Because
everybody was like, Gabriela,

00:49:22.600 --> 00:49:25.540
are you crazy to do statistics?
What are you going to do?

00:49:25.540 --> 00:49:27.175
Are you going to be
working in this--

00:49:28.555 --> 00:49:33.040
there was one unique, one only
place in Brazil where they

00:49:33.040 --> 00:49:36.000
hired statisticians.
It was a governmental

00:49:37.750 --> 00:49:39.340
thing and they were like, oh,

00:49:39.340 --> 00:49:40.930
are you going to
be working there?

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:44.280
So that was kind
of like, scary.

00:49:44.280 --> 00:49:45.790
But once I saw the other side

00:49:46.770 --> 00:49:50.370
that people were talking in
the news and the newspapers about

00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:55.380
data, statistics, and how
that influenced the decision makers

00:49:55.380 --> 00:49:56.970
and all of that.
And I'm like, oh,

00:49:56.970 --> 00:49:59.745
there is much more than
what people are telling me

00:50:01.170 --> 00:50:04.590
and that it's interesting,
that it's only like the math.

00:50:04.590 --> 00:50:07.830
But there is the other piece
about communication and

00:50:07.830 --> 00:50:09.150
decision-making

00:50:10.050 --> 00:50:11.700
and being a decision-maker

00:50:12.360 --> 00:50:13.830
that kind of like shifts

00:50:15.690 --> 00:50:20.910
my mindset as well. So that
was the turning point for me.

00:50:21.510 --> 00:50:21.645
-

00:50:21.645 --> 00:50:25.080
I was just double-checking to see
if there was a question on Slido.

00:50:25.080 --> 00:50:28.230
But something that we were
talking about before and I

00:50:28.230 --> 00:50:31.110
wanted to make sure we touched
upon it is we were talking about

00:50:32.030 --> 00:50:35.290
the process of hiring and
being a hiring manager,

00:50:35.970 --> 00:50:40.365
especially when you have a lot
